  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 201 PM EST Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A large area of high pressure will maintain generally \nquiet weather across the area through the end of the work week. \nTemperatures will run 5-10 degrees above normal through Friday. \nHighs will generally range through the 50s and lows will \nrange through the 30s into low 40s.  \n\nThe center of the surface high will remain to our south, which will \nmaintain a pressure gradient over our area that will support some \nlight west to southwest winds at the surface, with occasional gusts \nto around 15 mph possible each afternoon. Higher elevations, \nespecially over northern NY, will see higher gusts. Gusts over the \nnorthern Adirondacks will generally be in the 15 to 25 mph range, \nthough higher summits will see gusts in excess of 40 mph at times. \nThese breezes, along with some overnight cloud cover as shortwaves \nmove through, will prevent ideal radiational cooling conditions and \nwill keep lows seasonably mild.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Dry conditions are near certain to continue through \nSunday morning, with near record daily high temperatures anticipated \nat long term climate sites. Sunday will mark six consecutive warm \ndays across the region. Thereafter, increasing chances of rain, \nespecially beginning Sunday evening into Monday, will be tied to two \nsystems that will generally converge towards our region. Through \nearly next week, while the risk of impactful weather is low, a more \nactive pattern will begin regardless of how things play out. \n\nThere are generally three scenarios that model solutions fall into \nfor this period; a mainly dry one, a light rain event with perhaps \nsome higher terrain snow showers in the wake of a cold front, or a \nmoderate rainfall favored in southern/eastern portions of the \nregion. This latter, more noteworthy outcome would occur if low \npressure developing later this week forms farther west than the \nconsensus across Florida, which leads to a track hugging the east \ncoast rather than out to sea. The latest run of the GEFS strongly \nfavors an out to sea scenario, but its operational run and many \nmembers of the ECMWF EPS and AIFS systems still show a risk of this \nwetter scenario. \n\nThe most likely outcome is in the official forecast, which shows \nsome rain showers associated with an incoming cold front from the \nwest that will put an end to our unseasonable warmth. The character \nof this front is hard to pin down right now; a sharper front could \ninduce some relatively heavy showers and a changeover to snow by \nTuesday morning. Otherwise, would expect no impacts with \nprecipitation amounts under 0.25\" and more likely under 0.1\" in most \nlocations. The final scenario is almost entirely dry with both a \nmiss in the coastal storm and a cold front that produces little \nprecipitation as low pressure weakens to our north as it passes \nthrough Monday.",
